WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.137 --> 00:00:01.980
- So, is it true that when you won,

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:02.813
I just found this out,

00:00:02.813 --> 00:00:05.070
that when you were nominated
for your first Tony Award,

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:06.270
you didn't even know what a Tony was?

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:08.310
- No, no. That's, that's a true story.

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:12.000
You know the Tony Awards are
the big, prestigious award

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:12.903
that they give every year.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:00:12.903 --> 00:00:16.230
- And you know, I got in
this business for work.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:18.030
You know, I wanted a job,

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:20.280
and I didn't go following award shows

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:21.360
and things of that nature, so,

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:23.340
I'm doing "Jesus Christ Superstar"

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:24.507
and the knock comes on the door,

00:00:24.507 --> 00:00:27.300
and the guy says, "Mr.
Vereen, you have an envelope."

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.490
I said, "Give me the
envelope." He goes like this.

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:31.350
I said, "Give me the envelope."

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:34.920
Man says, "No. This is The Envelope!"

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:37.158
I said, "Yeah, yeah. Just
give me that damn envelope!"

00:00:37.158 --> 00:00:38.490
(Kelly laughs)
(audience laughing)

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:39.390
- I take the envelope.
- It's mine!

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:40.800
- I'm reading it, right?

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.590
And I'm realizing it's
from a guy named Tony.

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:45.518
(audience chuckles)
(Kelly laughs)

00:00:45.518 --> 00:00:47.643
So, so I turned to this guy and I go,

00:00:48.847 --> 00:00:51.180
"Who's Tony?"
(Kelly giggles)

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:52.115
- And why does he love me so much?

00:00:52.115 --> 00:00:53.040
- "And, what does he want?"

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:54.360
And then I read it,

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:56.850
and found out these are my peers.

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:58.200
Who said you got something,

00:00:58.200 --> 00:00:59.730
and matter of fact I
have that letter today.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:01.920
There's tears on the, on the letter.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:02.753
- Oh, that's so cool.
- Yeah.

00:01:02.753 --> 00:01:04.860
'Cause you, you get it that, you know,

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:06.317
you're in this, you don't think about it

00:01:06.317 --> 00:01:08.550
'cause we do it because
we love what we do.

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:09.383
- Yeah!
- You know, you just,

00:01:09.383 --> 00:01:10.530
you know, what you do.
- And you're just hustling,

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:11.363
you're grinding, you're doing it.

00:01:11.363 --> 00:01:12.196
- It's part of you doing your thing,

00:01:12.196 --> 00:01:13.029
you're doing your thing.

00:01:13.029 --> 00:01:15.150
And then somebody says,
"Hey you got something."

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:16.590
Now I'm just a kid from Brooklyn.

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:19.830
I was like, "Wow, they like me?"

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:21.180
- Oh that, it is cool.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:23.174
It's not about the
awards, I always say that.

00:01:23.174 --> 00:01:24.240
- [Ben] No it's not.
- Like, it's not about it,

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:26.400
but it is validating to like,

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.260
you know, do all this hard work,

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:30.090
and then somebody'd be like,
"That was pretty good."

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:30.923
- Yeah, yeah.
- That's nice.

00:01:30.923 --> 00:01:33.690
- Yeah, your peers saying,
"Yeah, you got something there."

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:36.510
- Did you always wanna do
Broadway? Were you always?

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:38.789
- Uh, I wanted to do employment.

00:01:38.789 --> 00:01:40.574
(Kelly laughs)
You know?

00:01:40.574 --> 00:01:41.700
- Me too.
- Yeah, yeah?

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:42.634
- I was looking to pay that electric bill.

00:01:42.634 --> 00:01:46.230
- I didn't, I really didn't set out

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:48.540
in my beginning to do Broadway.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:50.910
'Cause I didn't know what
Broadway was all about.

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:51.990
I'm this kid from Brooklyn

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:53.430
running around the streets, you know,

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:55.350
learning how to dance and
running from the cops.

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:57.650
You know? (chuckles)

00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:58.846
Somebody knows, yeah.
- It's a different movie?

00:01:58.846 --> 00:02:00.210
(both chuckling)
- Somebody knows.

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:03.397
And somebody said to me one day,

00:02:03.397 --> 00:02:05.817
"Why don't you go to the High
School for Performing Arts?"

00:02:05.817 --> 00:02:07.890
And I said, "Okay, cool."
I said, "Where is it?"

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:09.270
He said, "It's in Manhattan."

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:10.740
In those days you said Manhattan,

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:12.480
I thought I needed a passport

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:13.950
to get over to Manhattan.
(Kelly giggles)

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:15.570
I mean I was like, "Oh, okay."

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:16.920
And I would go to the school,

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:18.780
and I see dancers for the first time.

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:23.700
And I was like, "Wow, wow!
There's another world out here.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:25.343
It's called the Arts."

00:02:25.343 --> 00:02:26.550
I fell in love.
- And it's called opportunity

00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:28.423
too, for kids that love the Arts you know?

00:02:28.423 --> 00:02:29.857
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.
- That's a great opportunity.

00:02:29.857 --> 00:02:30.884
Not every kid gets that.

00:02:30.884 --> 00:02:32.490
- I had some great people to teach me,

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:34.560
and learned from some fantastic people.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:37.620
A woman named, Dr. Rachael Yocom,

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:39.510
was the principal in the dance department.

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:41.580
She saw me there with all these dancers

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:45.090
and I was just sitting there
with my Bermuda shorts on,

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:47.670
and my US kid sneakers, and my t-shirt,

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:49.650
and a little skinny-brim
hat, and I was sitting,

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:52.230
and made up a little dance to
"Killer Joe" by Quincy Jones.

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:54.330
I'm sitting there, get
up and do my little dance

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:56.995
and I get up in front of the
people and I do my little

00:02:56.995 --> 00:02:59.370
♪ Daaa-da-daa-da-daa-da-da ♪

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:01.020
I thought it was very cool, right?

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:02.940
But I didn't know the
people sitting at the table

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:04.290
were people like Martha Graham

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:05.760
and George Balanchine,

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:06.593
and all the greats of that time.

00:03:06.593 --> 00:03:07.860
- Sometimes ignorance is bliss.

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:08.693
- I didn't know who they were,

00:03:08.693 --> 00:03:09.630
they were just a bunch of white people.

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:11.005
- Yeah (cackles).
(audience laughs)

00:03:11.005 --> 00:03:12.929
- I was just doing my thing, you know?

00:03:12.929 --> 00:03:13.762
- Who are these people?
- And I come off stage

00:03:13.762 --> 00:03:16.620
and she said, "How'd you do?"
I said, "I did a'ight."

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:18.510
She said, "What's your name?"
I said, "Benjamin."

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:20.760
She said, "What?"
I said, "Benjamin."

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.124
She said, "You mean Benjamin?"

00:03:22.124 --> 00:03:23.919
I said, "Yeah, Benjamin.
What's wrong with you?"

00:03:23.919 --> 00:03:25.650
- (chuckles) Do you, do you hear me?

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:27.900
- Yeah, I tried to.
- Is this thing on?

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:28.770
- She said, "Would you
like to go to school?"

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:31.350
I said, "A'ight!"
- A'ight? (laughs)

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:33.684
- Here I am, folks. (chuckles)
- I love that!

00:03:33.684 --> 00:03:34.517
(audience applauding and cheering)

00:03:34.517 --> 00:03:36.750
One of the things that
you're best known for

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:39.270
is your role in the miniseries "Roots."

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:40.920
- Yes.
- And, I mean,

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:42.420
that changed people's lives.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:43.350
Now, I will tell you this,

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:44.880
I didn't tell you this when I saw you.

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:47.430
But like, that is the,

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:49.740
that is how I found out about slavery.

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:51.960
- [Ben] Really?
- And it was horrible.

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:52.830
It was in sixth grade,

00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:54.547
it was in Mr. Pesnell's
class, and he showed us.

00:03:54.547 --> 00:03:55.950
It was like science class.

00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:56.880
I didn't even know what we were watching,

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:58.560
but he played it and we watched it,

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:02.010
and I was just, it destroyed me.

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:03.420
It was like, I think, I feel like it was

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:05.910
the day that like the
whole innocence was gone

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:07.650
'cause I was like, "Oh,
there's like pure evil."

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:08.610
- Yeah.
- Like there's,

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:10.590
and I had no idea before that,

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:11.760
that that existed.

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:13.650
- Well, what's so sad is that slavery

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:15.720
still goes on today.
- I know.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:18.360
- And it was so wonderful to
be able to be a part of that.

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:21.390
I remember when Alex wrote that book.

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:23.910
I was, I used to go out with
a singer, song and dance man,

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:26.310
which, I still consider
myself a song and dance man.

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:31.310
And I heard about this
book, and I called my agent.

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:34.050
I said, "I wanna be a part of this book."

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:35.790
Because when I was growing up in school

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:37.230
there was only one paragraph,

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:39.090
you were a slave and Lincoln freed you.

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:41.190
I said, "No, that's not the whole truth,

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:42.690
and this guy has written the truth."

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:45.270
He went back to his home in Africa

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:48.186
and came across on a freighter

00:04:48.186 --> 00:04:50.670
to assimilate what it must have been like

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:52.320
to be in that ship.

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:54.690
And I said, "I wanna be
a part of this story,

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:56.370
to tell our history."

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:58.657
And so anyway, he said, he
looked at me and he said,

00:04:58.657 --> 00:05:02.310
"Ben," he said, "you're,
you, oh come on, man!

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:05.550
You're a song and dance man.
They're looking for actors."

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:08.430
He said, "Don't, don't
even think about it."

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:11.760
So I go to Chicago, and I'm
doing my show in Chicago

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:12.780
and there was opening,

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:15.199
a group was opening for
it called Sister Sledge.

00:05:15.199 --> 00:05:16.032
- Yeah. Oh, Yeah!
- These little girls

00:05:16.032 --> 00:05:16.980
with their song.

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:19.650
And so I go down to Savannah,
Georgia to do the show,

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:20.970
and they used to do a
character called black,

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:22.800
in blackface called Bert Williams.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:23.940
Which is a true story,

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:26.640
Black people weren't
allowed on stage in America

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:28.920
unless we blacked up, at
one point, in America.

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:30.390
- Yeah.
- So I was telling our story,

00:05:30.390 --> 00:05:32.400
and after the show was over,

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:35.257
this guy comes backstage
and he says to me,

00:05:35.257 --> 00:05:39.660
"Uh, we're here in
Savannah shooting 'Roots,'

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:42.240
and I want you to be my Chicken George."

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:43.710
And that's how I got the show.

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:45.540
- That's ah, that, it's-
(audience clapping)

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:48.420
it's just such an amazing thing

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:50.010
to love acting, to love what you do,

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:51.480
but to also be a part of something

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:52.313
that's-
- That's history.

00:05:52.313 --> 00:05:53.507
- so historical-
- It touches people's lives.

00:05:53.507 --> 00:05:54.771
- that's so, and it
changes people's lives.

00:05:54.771 --> 00:05:55.650
- Touches peoples lives.

00:05:55.650 --> 00:05:56.850
It does!
- For better or worse,

00:05:56.850 --> 00:05:58.467
you know, 'cause it
definitely destroyed me.

00:05:58.467 --> 00:06:02.070
- And for me, I got
the opportunity, Kelly,

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:06.030
to go back to Gambia, and
actually stand in the cell

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:09.510
where Black people were
enslaved and caged.

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:12.600
I mean, this, the cage is
no bigger than right here.

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:14.910
And hundreds of people,
sitting in there like this,

00:06:14.910 --> 00:06:15.870
were stuffed in there.

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:18.030
It was called the door of no return.

00:06:18.030 --> 00:06:20.880
At that time was it
called St. James Island.

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:22.950
Because of an actor who goes down,

00:06:22.950 --> 00:06:25.170
a painter went down and
talked to the government,

00:06:25.170 --> 00:06:27.240
they changed it to Kunta Kinte Island.

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:29.640
That's where they called
it the door of no return.

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:33.690
Where we, and I stood in the
cell where Kunta Kinte stood.

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:36.660
And I put my hand on the
wall and tears came down.

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:39.690
I said, "This is my wailing wall, Lord."

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:42.840
This is where we, cried and, you know,

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:44.070
it's where we suffered.

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:45.810
And it was quite an experience.

00:06:45.810 --> 00:06:47.040
I think everyone should,

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:49.500
if you have the opportunity, to do that.

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:52.920
I mean, my Jewish brothers
and sisters do it in Israel.

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:56.460
But in Gambia, right there
on Kunta Kinte Island,

00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:58.350
for us African Americans,

00:06:58.350 --> 00:07:00.484
the wailing wall, right there.

00:07:00.484 --> 00:07:02.370
- And I can't even imagine

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:03.840
like what that, what it felt like.

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:05.580
- And may you never have to imagine.

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:08.440
And may our children never
have to imagine again.

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:10.230
- Yeah. And you know-
- Again.

00:07:10.230 --> 00:07:11.580
- You know how-
- You know, it's important

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:15.360
that we, that we realize

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:17.760
this thing is not about you being Black,

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:19.470
you being white, you being yellow, red.

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:21.300
No! It's a God thing.

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:25.080
You see God's bouquet is all
sorts of beautiful colors.

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:26.670
And once we realize that,

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:29.340
we'll begin to realize
the divinity within us

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:31.020
and the love that exists.

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:33.090
- Yeah, I love that, God's bouquet.

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:35.220
- Yes! We're all God's bouquet.

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:36.240
There's no difference,

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:38.250
I mean, we are breathing the same air,

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:40.680
and call it God, Allah, Buddha, Jesus,

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:42.720
whatever you wanna call the Great Divine,

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:45.630
it exists, but only exists
when you recognize it,

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:47.520
and you and me, you and me.

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:49.048
- Absolutely.
- You and me.

00:07:49.048 --> 00:07:51.210
(audience clapping)
- And I wanted to say, too,

00:07:51.210 --> 00:07:52.740
it was amazing 'cause it,

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:55.140
it taught like, for such
a young kid like me,

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:57.591
and I think all kids, honestly,

00:07:57.591 --> 00:07:58.860
it's a tragic thing to watch,

00:07:58.860 --> 00:07:59.835
but it's a tragic thing that happened, so.

00:07:59.835 --> 00:08:00.668
- Oh, you must watch it.

00:08:00.668 --> 00:08:01.501
You must watch it.
- But you have to,

00:08:01.501 --> 00:08:02.610
and it, and it was cool 'cause it,

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:04.710
it actually taught me compassion, empathy,

00:08:04.710 --> 00:08:05.611
all these things.
- Yes.

00:08:05.611 --> 00:08:07.260
- You know, not just the negative things.

00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:08.167
It was all these things of like,

00:08:08.167 --> 00:08:09.480
"Oh my gosh, this can exist."

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:10.313
- Yes!
- And it's good

00:08:10.313 --> 00:08:11.463
that you see that, so we don't repeat it.

00:08:11.463 --> 00:08:13.170
- Yes, yes.
- And we don't continue it

00:08:13.170 --> 00:08:14.003
where it's happening now.

00:08:14.003 --> 00:08:14.977
- I constantly say,

00:08:14.977 --> 00:08:17.160
"If you don't know where you
came from, you don't know

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:18.180
where you're going."
- Where you're going, yeah.

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:20.550
- You might repeat the same
thing over and over again.

00:08:20.550 --> 00:08:22.170
And a lot of that's happening today.

00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:24.253
- That's insanity. (laughs)
- Today, it's happening today

00:08:24.253 --> 00:08:27.600
because we forgot who we are.

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:30.300
You see, we think we're
human beings having,

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:32.460
seeking for a spiritual experience.

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:36.480
No, we're spiritual beings
having a human experience.

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:37.890
- Oh, my. Write that book!

00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:38.723
- [Ben] Ah!
- Yeah!

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:41.160
- You know, once you realize-
- Oh, my God!

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:43.185
- that we're all spiritual beings

00:08:43.185 --> 00:08:45.180
having a human experience,

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:47.250
then we begin to recognize each other

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:49.470
as spiritual beings.

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:51.120
And stop looking at each other as, as,

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:54.510
as race or, or different genders.

00:08:54.510 --> 00:08:56.730
No, you're my brother, you're my sister,

00:08:56.730 --> 00:08:58.110
you're me, I'm you.

00:08:58.110 --> 00:09:01.980
I see you, I see me in
your eyes. We are one.

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:02.813
- I love that.
(audience clapping)

00:09:02.813 --> 00:09:06.310
- We are one.
(audience cheering)

